71 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(11)$ given below, compute $P(X<10).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ | $13$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.1432\\,$ | $\\,0.2320\\,$ | $\\,0.3405\\,$ | $\\,0.4599\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.6887\\,$ | $\\,0.7813\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167151 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "69667702-28d4-55ff-ad76-b5f0a166efd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167151 #edb/ref 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<10) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,9\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 9) \\\\  & =F(9) \\\\  & =0.3405.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167152 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167152 #edb/ref 1069 "q-224486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167152 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167152 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(6)$ given below, compute $P(2<X\\le 5).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0620\\,$ | $\\,0.1512\\,$ | $\\,0.2851\\,$ | $\\,0.4457\\,$ | $\\,0.6063\\,$ | $\\,0.7440\\,$ | $\\,0.8472\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167152 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74407edc-ca03-5605-ae34-cdfeb4fc24d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167152 #edb/ref 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(5) & =P(X\\le 5) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2\\})+P(X\\in \\{3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 2)+P(2<X\\le 5) \\\\  & =F(2)+P(2<X\\le 5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X\\le 5) & =F(5)-F(2) \\\\  & =0.4457-0.0620 \\\\  & =0.3837.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167558 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167558 #edb/ref 1061 221725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167558 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7a435309-f7d2-561b-9e2d-96419cf972f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167558 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167559 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167559 #edb/ref 1061 221726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167559 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "33739b57-c32a-5436-8a2f-c7b0a78550cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167559 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167560 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167560 #edb/ref 1061 221729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167560 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eb901a88-539f-5c1d-8161-d1cc6b32b4bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167560 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167561 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167561 #edb/ref 1061 221732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167561 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c2b9888b-da36-5ea0-907a-42747b8715c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167561 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167562 #edb/ref 1061 221735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167562 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ad4c4a48-5234-5c54-86de-eb13ccd09abb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167562 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168157 #edb/ref 1064 "When performing certain types of statistical tests (e.g., hypothesis testing), we often need to use standard cumulative distribution function (CDF) tables to carry out the test.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to construct CDF tables for the Poisson distribution and use them to calculate probabilities.\n\nFirst, recall that the probability mass function of the Poisson distribution $\\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,\\ldots\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ is a positive parameter.\n\nThe CDF of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's compute the values of $F(x)$ for the distribution $\\textrm{Po}(2)$ at $x=0$ and $x=1.$  \n\nTo do that, we use the definition of the cumulative distribution function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =P(X\\le 0) \\\\  & =P(X=0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{0}\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{0!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{1} \\\\  & ≈0.1353 \\\\ F(1) & =P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{0}\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{0!}+\\frac{{2}^{1}\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{1!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{1}+\\frac{2\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{1} \\\\  & ≈0.4060\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe CDF data is usually presented as a table. If we were to continue this process and calculate $F(2), F(3), F(4),$ and $F(5),$ our table would look as follows:\n\n| $\\quad x \\quad$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\leq x)$ | $\\,0.1353\\,$ | $\\,0.4060\\,$ | $\\,0.6767\\,$ | $\\,0.8571\\,$ | $\\,0.9473\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\cdots\\,\\,\\,$ |\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $F(x)$ is an increasing function.\n\n- Since $\\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$ takes arbitrarily large values, we have $F(x) < 1$ for all $n,$ and $F(x) \\to 1$ as $x\\to\\infty.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168157 #edb/ref 1065 9443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168157 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7f81a6db-33d2-5a6a-98e7-852037072330" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168157 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502972 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186503771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502972 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186503803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502972 #edb/ref 